Testing Individual Components

> Ultrasonic Sensor: We tested the ultrasonic sensor by holding it and moving it closer and farther from a bucket filled with water. The sensor successfully detected the changes in distance, showing that it was working properly.

> LEDs: We tested the LEDs separately using the 9V battery. It lighted up correctly, confirming that they were functional and properly connected.

> Buzzer: We tested the buzzer by connecting it to the circuit and placing an object within the set distance. The buzzer produced sound as expected, indicating that it was working and responding properly to the sensor’s signal.

> LCD Display: We tested the LCD Display by uploading a simple display program. The screen successfully showed the measured distance and status readings by confirming proper communication with the Arduino.

>MOSFET and Water Pump: We verified that the MOSFET and water pump by triggering the control signal from the Arduino. The pump turned on and off correctly, showing that the MOSFET and pump worked properly.

> Power Supply: The 9V battery provided stable voltage to all components during operation, ensuring that the system functioned properly throughout testing.

Testing the Complete System
> Water Level Simulation: The ultrasonic sensor accurately measured the water height in the container, the LED indicators displayed safe (0–2 cm below), warning (3 cm), and critical (4 cm above) levels, and the buzzer sounded for 10 seconds when the water reached the danger threshold. When the water level reached the critical point, the pump automatically started drawing water, and it continued operating until the reading reached 0.0 cm, at which point the pump automatically stopped. Although minor inaccuracies occurred when the water surface was disturbed, the system functioned reliably and effectively overall.

> System Stability: We conducted multiple trials to assess the system’s stability, ensuring that all components, including sensors, LEDs, buzzer, and pump, operated synchronously and consistently under continuous operation.

> Flood Control Simulation: We verified that the system effectively simulated real-time flood control by automatically managing water levels and preventing overflow.

Troubleshooting Challenges
During the testing phase, several minor issues were encountered, including difficulty connecting the Arduino to the water pump.

The water pump failed to operate while the LED indicators remained functional. This issue was resolved by integrating a MOSFET and using an additional 9V battery to facilitate proper water pumping.

Troubleshooting was required for the PCB because the purchased board was incorrect, and the traces on the back of the PCB did not properly connect the components. Adjustments were made to ensure all connections were correctly linked.

When the ultrasonic sensor showed incorrect measurements, it was adjusted to ensure accurate detection of the water height as it gradually increased during testing.
Water leakage was observed in the pathways of the assembled model, which was resolved by applying additional sealing to ensure stable water measurement during continuous trials.


Final Validation

The flood control system was tested multiple times at 2816 Molave St., Abegail’s residence, under simulated flood conditions to assess its overall performance. During the testing phase, varying water levels were introduced to evaluate the system’s ability to detect and respond to changes in real time. The results confirmed that the system accurately measured the water height using the ultrasonic sensor, activated the corresponding LED indicators to represent safe, warning, and critical levels, and triggered the buzzer for 10 seconds once the danger threshold was reached. The water flow along the sides of the container was also monitored to verify the stability and consistency of sensor readings.

It was noted, however, that slight deviations in measurement occurred when the water surface was disturbed. This behavior is attributed to the ultrasonic sensor’s reliance on stable surface reflections for precise distance measurement, which can be temporarily affected by surface movement or turbulence. Once the water surface became calm, the sensor readings returned to their accurate state.

In summary, the results demonstrated that the flood control system functioned effectively and reliably under controlled conditions. Minor variations in readings due to water surface disturbances were identified as an expected sensor characteristic and did not significantly impact the system’s overall accuracy or performance.

